  "preamble": "The vowel [e] shifts to [a] through lowering and backing, a vowel shift pattern attested in various language families. In Chukchi-Kamchatkan languages, this represents a synchronic vowel harmony alternation where mid front vowel /e₂/ alternates with low back vowel /a/ depending on the presence of 'dominant' vowels (back/lower vowels) in the word.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lowering, Backing, Vowel Harmony",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Chukchi",
      "languageFamily": "Chukotko-Kamchatkan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "/e₂/ (recessive)",
          "to": "[a] (in dominant vowel context)",
          "note": "Chukchi vowel harmony: 'recessive' vowel /e₂/ lowers and backs to [a] when a 'dominant' vowel (back or lower vowels /a, o, e₂/ or /u/) appears in the word. Height-based harmony system where dominants override recessives. This is a synchronic process, not historical change, maintaining allophonic variation within paradigms."
        }
      ]
